<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Masmughans of Damavand</h1><p class="st-meta">651 CE – 760 CE (approx.) · capital Damavand</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Zoroastrian lords of Mount Damavand whose title meant 'great magus', holding out against the Arab conquest until 760.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Masmughans ruled the district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damavand">Damavand</a> in the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alborz">Alborz</a>, east of later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>. Their title, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Middle Persian</a> mas-mughan, 'great of the magi', suggests a priestly origin. After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> fell, they reached terms with Arab commanders around 651 and kept their autonomy.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Little is known of their rule. They governed a mountain population that remained Zoroastrian, paid tribute to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> and allied with the neighbouring <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dabuyid-dynasty">Dabuyid dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a>. The fortress region around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-damavand">Mount Damavand</a>, the peak of Iranian legend, gave them protection.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>During the Abbasid conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a> in 759 to 760, the last Masmughan was defeated and captured, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damavand">Damavand</a> passed under direct caliphal rule.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/masmughans-of-damavand.jpg" alt="Masmughans of Damavand" width="320" height="250"><figcaption>Image: Society for the Diffusion of Useful Knowledge (Great Britain) Engraver or Printer Murchison, I. Engraver or Printer L... · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Persia_with_part_of_the_Ottoman_Empire,_1872.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>651 CE – 760 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Damavand</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Zoroastrianism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Masmughan, Masmughans</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=706&amp;focus=masmughans-of-damavand" aria-label="Masmughans of Damavand on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5994 880 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Masmughans of Damavand lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6314 1136L6134 1224"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6134 1224h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6314 1136h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6314 1136h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Damavand. <a href="/atlas?year=706&amp;focus=masmughans-of-damavand">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

